   "text": {
    "L1": "Land can change even when we cannot see it happening. These changes are usually very slow.",
    "L2": "Weathering means rocks slowly break down where they are. Erosion means broken rock and soil are carried away.",
    "L3": "Freezing water widens cracks, plant roots push into cracks, and heating and cooling can break rock apart.",
    "L4": "Flowing water, wind, moving ice, and sea waves can carry the broken material away. That carrying is erosion.",
    "L5": "In a mountain stream, pieces knock and rub against one another and the riverbed. Their sharp edges wear down into rounded pebbles.",
    "L6": "Over a very long time, the pieces can become fine sand. When the river slows near the sea, it drops the sand.",
    "L7": "Soil forms from weathered rock mixed with humus. It takes hundreds of years to form a few centimetres.",
    "L8": "Tree roots and grass hold soil and protect it from erosion. A bare slope loses more soil when water flows over it.",
    "L9": "So remember: weathering breaks rock in place, while erosion carries the pieces away. Which process matches each label?"
   },
